WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.860
A work factor is a setting that influences the computational cost of password hashing.

2
00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:15.720
Higher cost can improve resistance to offline guessing after hash exposure, but it also consumes more server time and sometimes more memory during legitimate authentication.

3
00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:18.620
The right setting is not simply the highest number possible.

4
00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:26.240
It must balance user experience, server capacity, concurrency, password reset behavior, and availability.

5
00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:31.400
Login latency is the most visible cost, but it is not the only one.

6
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:41.180
Account creation, password reset, forced password changes, background account migrations, and bursty login events can all trigger hashing work.

7
00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:47.680
If cost is tuned without considering peak traffic, the authentication system may become slow or unstable.

8
00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:52.580
If cost is too low, exposed hashes may be easier to attack offline.

9
00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:57.400
Good tuning uses measurement, capacity planning, and periodic review.

10
00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:04.640
Denial-of-service considerations matter because attackers may try to force the application to spend resources on expensive password checks.

11
00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:12.980
Rate limiting, throttling, progressive delays, lockout tradeoffs, and bot or abuse controls at a high level help protect availability.

12
00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:19.400
Lockouts must be designed carefully because overly aggressive lockouts can be used to disrupt legitimate users.

13
00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:24.940
The defensive goal is to reduce automated abuse without creating a new operational weakness.

14
00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:33.780
Monitoring authentication performance helps teams detect when cost settings, traffic patterns, or infrastructure changes create risk.

15
00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:40.760
Track login latency, error rates, reset activity, throttle events, and unusual spikes.

16
00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:52.460
Password hashing should be expensive enough to reduce damage after database exposure, but operationally sustainable enough that users can authenticate reliably and defenders can identify abuse quickly.
